Dear All3DP Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is a desktop application for people who
want to adapt a downloaded 3D-printing model or build a printable part from
dimensions, sketches and tested components without learning a full CAD system
for every small change.

Here is the clearest story your readers can test in five minutes: download a
useful STL, click an existing hole, and change its diameter without finding
the source CAD file or rebuilding the part.

That is one of the headline additions in Solidon3D 0.3.0. The application
recognises a hole in an imported mesh, preserves its position and axis, and
replaces the diameter as a reversible operation. It also works on angled
holes. A reader can turn an M4 mount into M5, add a heat-set insert from a
standards-based parts catalogue, and later change both steps again.

Since the first public 0.1 demo, Solidon has accumulated 220 curated,
user-visible changes rather than a padded commit count. The largest additions
include:

* reusable parametric parts built from history steps without code;
* sketching and pull-to-height previews directly in the 3D view;
* printable nuts, screws and bearing seats with matching dimensions;
* per-spool colour and print settings carried into 3MF and the slicer;
* Windows, macOS and Linux builds, with no account or cloud storage.

Solidon is aimed at the gap between “I found a model” and “the part fits,” not
at replacing a full CAD system. The complete demo is free until 30 October
2026; version 1.0 follows as a one-time purchase, not a subscription.

Demo and screenshots: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Full changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

Reply “test” and I will send the 0.3.0 build, the original STL, exact
before-and-after measurements, and publication-ready images. There are no
conditions on the verdict.

